  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Introduction\n\nA Least Recently Used (LRU) cache is a memory-efficient caching strategy that evicts the item that has not been accessed for the longest period when the cache reaches its capacity. Python's `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation for implementing this data structure because it maintains insertion order and supports moving items to the end, which directly maps to the \"most recently used\" semantics.\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU cache using OrderedDict.\n    \n    - Most recently accessed items stay in the cache.\n    - Least recently accessed items are evicted when capacity is exceeded.\n    - All operations (get, put) are O(1) average time complexity.\n    \n    Attributes:\n        capacity (int): Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n        cache (OrderedDict): Stores key-value pairs in order of recent use.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache with a given capacity.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can store.\n                       Must be a positive integer.\n            \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than or equal to zero.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Cache capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        \n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache: OrderedDict[Any, Any] = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key: Any) -> Optional[Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the given key.\n        \n        If the key exists in the cache, it is marked as most recently used\n        and its value is returned. If the key does not exist, None is returned.\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n            \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or None if the key is not found.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move the accessed key to the end to mark it as most recently used\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key: Any, value: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a",
